[Music]  this morning on the dish a milestone for  one of the nation's largest restaurant  groups  rich mel-min opened his first venue  chicago's rj grunts 50 years ago  since then his lettuce entertain you  dining group has grown to include  60 brands and 130 restaurants across  more than  a dozen states i sat down with mel-min  in that first restaurant  on a street that now bears his name to  learn the secrets of his company's  success  i mean is this what it looked like this  was it yeah this was it  the walls are the same the floor is the  same it didn't change much  chicago's rj grunts opened in june of  1971.  it was just a hip casual fun type of  place that drew  a terrific crowd  50 years later the memories are still  vivid for co-founder rich mellman  it was his first restaurant with partner  jerry orzoff  and you never forget your first it's  very special the first one is very it's  like your first love  and for mel-min quite literally where he  fell  in love with his wife of 45 years martha  who joined us as we reminisced on a  recent visit i was standing here and  jerry came up to me  and said you know you're so crowdy said  anybody you want to meet  yet you know i said there's a girl  standing right over there you see that  one  and i'd walk around r.j grunts in the  early days  and if there was a cute girl there i'd  remember what she ate  more than i'd remember her do you  remember what she ate that night  i don't i really don't and he wasn't the  only one  and it kind of must have been true love  because i have not a clue what i ate  before starting then raising his own  family in the restaurant business  mel-min grew up in it his mom was a  waitress his dad a restaurant owner  do you remember the moment when you  realized that you wanted to go into the  restaurant business  yeah it certainly was pushed along by me  flunking out of college  so i fell back into working in  restaurants  often you know when you get good at  something you wind up liking it  and i i got real good at it hopefully we  could have a party  mel-min worked for his dad and saved his  money but when he tried to buy into the  business  his dad turned him down mel-min soon set  off on his own  but struggled to find investors until a  friend  suggested jerry orzoff his mother was  also a waitress  and we talked about how rough  they had had it and we said when we were  dreaming about the type of restaurant  we'd like to do let's make it the type  of place  where our moms would have felt great to  work at  the duo would spend the next decade  developing restaurants together  even after orzoff's death in 1981 the  culture he'd created with mel-min  lived on in the let us entertain you  brand there's a very important word  that's caring  and it's caring about the customer  caring about the food  caring about the service and a very  important thing is caring about yourself  we coach a lot you know you have to be  nurturing  and i like to think that's what we're  like as an organization we're nurturing  still based in chicago it's nearly  impossible to walk the streets there and  not stumble into a lettuce entertaining  restaurant  with 130 nationwide and 60 concepts  there's a wide variety of food in the  portfolio  with some of mel-min's favorites  tempting us during our interview  over there by you that's muhammara it's  a turkish  dish pulled chicken nachos one of the  biggest sellers and this is a prawn dish  prawns and lemon and some herbs  this is a carrot cake that martha  experimented with over the years and we  served it at rj's first birthday  and the kids wanted to put it in their  first restaurant  and i said oh i'm not sure if it'll be a  big seller  so much i know it's the biggest seller  they have for dessert  that first restaurant for the kids was  hub 51  launched by sons rj and jared who along  with their sister molly  worked their way up in the family  business rj now serving as president  molly and jared executive partners  what's the best piece of advice  that your dad has given you as you move  forward in this industry  something he always said growing up was  find your passion and then work your  hardest at it he never put  a lot of pressure on us to be in the  business you know you don't always have  to be the best at  anything but you can always hustle when  you're not trying your best is when  you're not going to be successful  i think he's great at always pushing us  to try to make things  even better even when things are going  great he always says there's there's  room to  improve it's really tender i don't know  if you just had to bite the head  yeah it's like this isn't spicy to me  okay good  for you to have your children in your  business  what does that mean what a blessing if  they  didn't have the talent didn't have the  drive didn't have the confidence to do  it  i would talk to them about trying  something else but make no mistake  mel-min isn't done personally helping to  build the let us entertain you legacy  i tried retirement i didn't enjoy it  take sundays off i  take vacations but i think  it would be hard for me to just not do  restaurants and  i don't have any other real hobbies you  love it i do i  love it and your kids are in it and it's  this is family yeah  and no surprise when reflecting on a  half-century anniversary  the man who started that family with  mel-min is very much  on his mind if you were having this meal  and you can have it with anyone past or  present  who would it be  jerry yeah my best friend  yeah i would love to um  share this with you to jerry for sure  so sweet danny this family was wonderful  the love between them  the energy the way they work together  and you can feel that in their  restaurants  we brought you the carrot cake from  martha okay and so  and cheers to let us entertain you in 50  years  exactly

